[Music]  the zelda series has come  a long way since its inception in terms  of combat  we went from stabbing to stabbing while  jumping and crouching  to swinging and performing sword dashes  or spin attacks to full-on 3d swordplay  with lots of different options in just a  span of a decade which  is quite impressive after the release of  ocarina of time however these leaps in  combat innovation  started to plateau and since then the  combat system hasn't seen  too many big overhauls well with the  exception of one particular game but we  will cover that one in a separate  section  on the one hand this isn't really a bad  thing it's inevitable that after  experimenting with different styles and  trying out different things you  ultimately land on something that is  most efficient and start making less  radical changes to the system but it's  not so much about nintendo adding to the  combat than it is about them  taking things away on the surface level  breath the wild's combat system seems  radically different because of the many  new additions like  flurry rushing and perfect guarding  bullet time and using the environment or  sheikah slay to your advantage  but in terms of actual one-to-one  swordplay not that  much has changed in fact in a lot of  ways it's actually watered down compared  to previous installments like twilight  princess but  i'm getting ahead of myself in this  video i want to take a closer look at  how the combat system of previous games  measure up to that of the latest  installment that being breathed the wild  and see what kind of improvements can be  made in the sequel  whether it being a return to form or  completely new additions  of course since we are dealing with  three dimensions here i will limit the  scope of this video to the 3d  installments in the series  since the 2d games have a very different  and more simplistic approach to combat  which suits those types of games  so let's get into it inside breath of  the wild 2.  wait damn it wrong channel let's start  at the beginning with ocarina of time  the first 3d installment which laid the  groundwork for all future titles to come  coming off of a link to the past  obviously this game had to completely  reinvent how combat was going to work as  opposed to a simple stab or swing link  was given numerous options to work with  horizontal swings vertical swings stabs  stabs while crouching and shielding  jump attacks spin attacks and even some  stuff that didn't necessarily add  anything significant but was still an  interesting addition  like being able to reverse your swinging  pattern by tilting the control stick in  the opposite direction of the first  swing  as has become tradition for the 3d  zeldas standard swings can be extended  to a three-part combo with the third  swing acting as the finisher and after  some cooldown it resets to the first  swing for each variation the finisher is  different  again this doesn't really change or  impact the combat in any way but it  makes the sword combat seem more varied  and complex than it really is  to complement all of this z targeting  made its debut as a way to focus your  attacks on a specific  enemy without losing track of its  position some attacks like the regular  stab  jump attack and reverse horizontal swing  cannot even be executed without it  z targeting also grants more defensive  options like walking while shielding  sidestepping and a backflip to dodge  specific attacks  i remember as a kid purposely drawing  out a fight to make it look like an  epic battle whenever someone was  watching me play some people seem to  agree with  sequelitis that z targeting was trash  and it created a disconnect between the  combat section and the exploration  section but i completely disagree on  that front  first of all in most cases you don't  have to target an enemy it just makes  the fights easier  in fact in some occasions it's better  not to see target because z targeting  while having your sword out takes away  the options to roll  instead replacing it with the jump  attack this elaborate combat system  especially for its time has influenced  many different games in the future to  come  including the zelda games following in  its footsteps majira's mask being  developed in only a year and on the  ocarina engine  understandably reused the exact same  system with the only difference being  that link now has different forms and  thus some attacks are aesthetically  different with punches and kicks being  added to the mix but the core system  remains largely the same  the wind waker saw the inclusion of  parry moves where you need to time a  press of the a button right before an  enemy attack to perform either  a helm splitter or a back slice although  i appreciate the inclusion  i'm not really the biggest fan of this  mechanic the reason being that this  parry system is limited to specific  enemy types and particularly the ones  that can only be beaten  using this technique in other words the  game decides for you whether or not you  can use it  it also makes some of the enemies like  dark nuts feel a little bit more  gimmicky as you simply wait for that big  flashy button accompanied with a loud  sound and immediately hit their weak  spots automatically  it also doesn't help that you don't have  any control over which type of parry  attack you use  the game simply chooses for you based on  what kind of attack the enemy is  performing  taking away any incentive or strategy  from the player and apparently nintendo  agreed with all of this since in  twilight princess this parry system was  done away with again  the helm splitter and backslides are  still a thing but in this case can be  triggered on command  the helm splitter after a shield bash  and the backslides upon landing after a  sidestep  and perhaps most famously for twilight  princess and a big fan favorite was the  addition of hidden skills  link's base moveset wasn't anything  special and almost identical to that of  ocarina of time  however this can be expanded upon by  learning various hidden skills from the  hero's shade  of which the aforementioned helm  splitter and backslides are also part of  with the exception of the ending blow  which you are forced to learn since  well it's used to kill ganondorf at the  end none of the other hidden skills are  mandatory to beat the game  this works really well as it can make  fighting certain enemies like again dark  nuts  easier to fight with the hidden skills  but not near impossible without them  and just as a side note twilight  princess has by far  the best underwater combat system in the  entire series  just throwing that out there another  thing to mention is the three-part combo  system from ocarina of time is once  again present in both the wind waker and  twilight princess  a nice little inclusion this time around  is that you can change between the  different swings mid  combo by tilting the control stick in  different directions between each attack  this can result in all kinds of  different swings and finisher  combinations  again this doesn't really impact the  outcome but it looks cool  and then we got skyward sword and it  sucked moving on alright i'm just  kidding  jokes aside though skyward swords combat  is one of the most controversial aspects  and the wii in general for that matter  some thoroughly enjoyed it while others  broke their tv sets  i fall somewhere in between on this  matter i can see why nintendo tried it  and for a game which made use of such an  experimental control scheme it performed  reasonably well  one big thumbs up i would give to  skyward sword is the fact that the  directions of your swings actually  matter this time around  something i was actually surprised  wasn't included earlier to some degree  in previous installments the direction  of your sword swing was pure aesthetics  and it didn't serve any other function  it had no impact on whether or not you  would land a hit or not  why the game gets a thumbs down however  is that it only works well with  certain types of enemies i think  nintendo was a little overzealous with  this mechanic  and implemented it for pretty much every  enemy in the game this turned a lot of  the combat in skyward sword into a slow  paced game of  guess the direction the enemy woke god  will he get it right folks let's see  oh that was a shocker if i've ever seen  one  [Music]  and even if you were seemingly swinging  in the right direction  some enemies like the mccobblens shift  their guard around so frequently and so  abruptly that in some cases you're  better off just swinging away in random  directions anyway  i think it would have been better if  this was reserved for only specific  fights  the novelty of swinging a sword in real  time was already enough to make it fun  if anything forcing this directional  swing mechanic on even the most basic  enemies takes away that enjoyment  the fights with girohim were fast paced  and exciting so it definitely wasn't a  complete failure  of course i cannot really use skyward  swords combat as an example for most  things related to breath the wild and  its sequel since it exists in its own  world  and given the controversy surrounding  the motion controls i highly doubt  nintendo is going back to this control  scheme anytime soon unless we maybe get  a zelda game in vr one day  as mentioned in the opening narrative  breath the wild's combat options overall  are  amazing when looking at the whole  picture but at the same time the actual  swordplay was heavily stripped down  which is quite ironic if you take into  account all the different weapons we're  allowed to use in this installment all  those different swing directions  finishers and options we talked about  earlier have been neglected and we are  now stuck with the same simple  three-swing combo for one-handed weapons  two  swings for two-handed ones and a  five-stab combo for spears and halberds  you can no longer influence the  direction or the finishing move which is  kind of boring if you ask me  i am of course aware that breath of wild  introduced many new things in the form  of the chica slate more emphasis on the  bow and generally using the environment  to your advantage  but let's be fair here outside of the  great plateau tutorial section and some  situational moments here and there to  clear out camps the only room to really  be effective in high-paced combat  scenarios was stasis plus  and maybe occasionally a bomb but good  luck trying to kill a lino with a  boulder or hit a lizzle foes with a  metal crate after it's already spotted  you besides we are primarily focusing on  sword combat here so here's what i would  change to the overall sword mechanics  for the sequel  first of all bring back the hidden  skills this is a no-brainer  having the option to expand your moveset  is always a good thing in my book  especially if it's handled like in  twilight princess and isn't mandatory if  it's not mandatory it won't get in the  way of the open nature of the game and  thus will not bother anyone who doesn't  feel like collecting everything  hidden skills are purely there to make  link more powerful and expand his  options but are not required to beat any  specific enemy  second expand the combos you can perform  with each weapon  i can't believe i'm making this  comparison but remember star fox  adventures aka the legend of fox  dinosaur planet where you could perform  all these flashy combinations and  finishers  sure the combat itself was quite  repetitive but  it was cool how you could make fox do  all kinds of different strings  ending with a jump attack a roundhouse  kick or spin his staff around like a  helicopter blade  say what you want about this game but  some of the moves you could perform were  pretty damn cool  and just like in the wind waker and  twilight princess you can alternate  directions on the control stick to  change the order of the combo  in breath of the wild all that stuff was  gone which is a pretty big step back if  you ask me  especially since this is the first game  in the series where we can use so many  different kinds of weapons  next make the enemy ai more varied in  combat scenarios  now i know what you're thinking the ai  was already pretty advanced for a zelda  game right especially the bokoblins and  moblins  in terms of their overall behavior yes  and the way they interacted with some of  the chica runes or the environment  definitely but in terms of actual  one-to-one duels  not so much it still boils down to  waiting  blocking and the occasional parry here  and there and just wailing on them like  there's no tomorrow there's also no way  to break an enemy's defense  other than bashing away at their shield  or waiting until they let their guard  down  this ties in with the hidden skills we  covered earlier in twilight princess and  skyward sword you could use the shield  bash to open an enemy up  and in the case of twilight princess use  the back slice or helm splitter to  circumvent the enemy's defense  in general i would love to see a more  unpredictable attack pattern where you  need to use different strategies to  outsmart an enemy which in the case of  breath of wild doesn't really happen  unless you're fighting a boss ultimately  one of the biggest reasons for a lack of  impressive sword duels is of course the  enemy variety  something that a lot of people seem to  agree with no dark nuts stalfos iron  knuckles  re-deads or any of the other classic  enemies can be found  which is a shame as fighting some of  these enemies was very entertaining  now this next one is a bit more of a  personal one so i'm curious to see how  many of you agree with me  be sure to let me know down in the  comments so here's my take  giving an enemy insane amounts of hp  isn't hard  it's tedious i am of course referring to  the color variations of each enemy and  in particular the silver  and if you're playing on hard mode the  golden types making a fight an  ordeal because it takes ages to chip  away at their health  creates the illusion of difficulty but  the fight itself  isn't actually challenging the same  strategy still apply  the only quote unquote challenge here is  not losing your focus and attention  because of the endless repetition of  doing the same thing  over and over it's a test of patience  more than it is a test of skill once  you've become good at the game and can  parry almost  any attack successfully trigger flurry  rushes and generally know the attack  pattern of each enemy you are left with  very little than spending a long  long time to take down an enemy hard  mode's idea of difficulty was to just  have enemies regenerate health  which i think is kind of a lazy solution  to up the difficulty this also clashes  heavily with the weapon durability  system  i've stated before in previous videos  that i don't really have a problem with  the durability  in fact i think it was kind of a nice  change of pace for the series and  incentivized exploration and testing out  different weapons  but burning through two-thirds of your  inventory or even more in the process of  bringing down a silver or golden lino is  a bit much if you ask me the reward  doesn't outweigh the sacrifice  either the durability of the weapons  needs to go up or even better  make the enemy type stronger by making  their behavior more unpredictable  expand their moveset with attacks which  the weaker variants don't use  it's fine if they have more hp but not  to this degree  that's just my opinion of course some  quick honorable mentions i would also  like to see  bring back some sort of roll mechanic  this doesn't have to replace the jump by  any means  but rolling has always been that little  extra option to shift your herd box and  dodge certain attacks  i especially miss this mechanic while  fighting certain enemies like stone  talus or a high knocks  it would be great to be able to roll  underneath them and attack them from  behind  and as we saw in the star fox example a  role could perhaps be  expanded with a hidden skill which  allows you to do a lunge attack right  after  additionally i would also love to see a  certain degree of aerial combat options  we've had a jump attack since ocarina of  time and breath the wild had this too  together with a sort of ground pound  type of move while paragliding but i'm  talking about  actual sword swings while jumping in the  air you know like we see link do in  super smash brothers  again i absolutely love the new  additions breath the wild brought in  when it comes to fighting enemies  but i do feel like nintendo stripped  away a little too much from the previous  titles when it comes to the flexibility  the sword has  having access to spears and all kinds of  other different weapons is great  however their use is quite limited aside  from the regular predetermined swings  throwing a weapon or a charge attack  link is a pretty agile and young dude so  he can get a bit more acrobatic with his  fighting style maybe he should take some  notes from monk moss koshia  for a 10 000 year old guy he's pretty  light on his feet  now it's hard to say exactly if nintendo  will make big changes to the combat  system in breath of the wild too  or if they will greatly expand the  amount of enemy types for that matter  the big question is of course what will  link's new magic hand abilities be used  for  as of right now besides speculation we  have no idea what the implications of  this new mechanic will be  nor do we even know if it will be used  in combat will it affect link's sword  wielding arm can we still use two-handed  weapons and can we use one-handed  weapons in conjunction with the green  arm  as of right now i don't want to get too  speculative about this mechanic in  today's video so for now all we can do  is wait until we actually see some  gameplay which i'm super hyped for  and i hope you are too and now it's time  to give you all an  update about my upcoming schedule  because the next few weeks are going to  be a bit different from the norm  starting this sunday i'll be leaving for  my annual hiking trip so i won't be  making any content  until august 8th however i'm planning to  record one small additional video  tomorrow if i can find a time  and i will schedule this video to go up  next weekend to fill in the gap  this video will not feature any high  quality editing or animations as i only  have one day to produce it  my big calamity video i decided to save  for after i come back  and finish it with a clear mindset since  as you can probably imagine i'm doing a  lot of preparation right now  this way i can also premiere the episode  properly august itself will be filled  with different kinds of projects a  couple of which will be collaborations  with other creators so i hope you look  forward to that i also have a couple of  new patreon supporters to announce so a  big warm welcome to william  morales lord gannon lauricia jarvis  jen joe and julia clemen thank you all  so much for joining  you guys rock with that said  i will see you all in roughly 2 weeks  and as always thank you for watching  this is dawn signing off and have a good  one

